{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-81775",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,x\\le y\\le 1-x\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}{\\int }_{x}^{1-x}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}{\\int }_{x}^{1-x}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{x}[{\\int }_{x}^{1-x}2y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{x}[{y}^{2}]_{x}^{1-x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{x}((1-x)^{2}-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{x}(1-2x)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}(\\frac{1}{x}-2)dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}(\\frac{1}{x}-2)dx & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2x]_{1/4}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-1-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
